{"question": "A client submits a passport that expired 4 months ago. Is this acceptable as a primary identity document?", "response": {"question": "A client submits a passport that expired 4 months ago. Is this acceptable as a primary identity document?", "answer": "Yes, if it was issued within the last 10 years and is otherwise clear and legible. Passports are accepted if valid or expired within the last 6 months.", "citations": [{"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 2. Acceptable Primary Identity Documents\n\nA primary identity document must be government-issued, photographic, and contain the holder's full legal name and date of birth. The following are accepted:\n\n| Document Type | Jurisdiction | Conditions |\n|---------------|-------------|------------|\n| Passport | Any | Valid, **or** expired within the last **6 months** |\n| National Identity Card | EU/EEA countries and UK | Must be valid at the time of submission |\n| Biometric Residence Permit | UK | Must be valid at the time of submission |\n| Driving Licence (photocard) | UK, EU/EEA, US, Canada, Australia | Must be valid; paper licences not accepted |\n\n**A document issued more than 10 years ago is not acceptable** as a primary identity document, even if it technically remains valid (this most commonly affects older passports with 10-year validity that are still in date but were issued more than 10 years ago).\n\nOnly one primary identity document is required. If the document is damaged, partially illegible, or of poor quality, the client must be asked to resubmit with a clearer copy or an alternative document.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Acceptable Proof of Address Documents"}, {"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 3. Acceptable Proof of Address Documents\n\nA proof of address document must show the client's full name and current residential address. It must have been **issued within the last 3 months** at the time of submission.\n\nThe following are accepted:\n\n- Bank or building society statement (original or electronic, from a regulated institution)\n- Utility bill (gas, electricity, water, broadband — mobile phone bills are not accepted)\n- Council tax bill (current year)\n- HMRC correspondence (e.g., tax return notice, NI correspondence)\n- Mortgage statement (from a regulated lender)\n- Tenancy agreement (signed and dated, covering the current period)\n\nA **mobile phone bill is not an acceptable proof of address** under any circumstances.\n\nThe proof of address must match the residential address declared by the client. Where there is a discrepancy, the reviewing officer must request an explanation and document it on the file.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Document Age and Validity Rules\n\nThe following rules apply regardless of document type:"}, {"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 4. Document Age and Validity Rules\n\nThe following rules apply regardless of document type:\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| Primary ID validity | Passport: valid or expired within 6 months. National ID / BRP / Driving Licence: must be valid at submission |\n| Primary ID issuance age | Must have been issued within the last 10 years |\n| Proof of address age | Must have been issued within the last 3 months |\n| Supporting SoW documents | Age requirements vary by document type — see Source of Wealth Verification Guidelines (Policy 02) |\n\nWhere a client is unable to provide documents meeting these requirements, the reviewing officer must escalate to the MLRO before proceeding.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Remote Onboarding and Biometric Verification\n\nAll clients onboarding through the platform's remote digital channel are subject to biometric identity verification. This requirement cannot be waived for remote clients.\n\nBiometric verification must:"}], "model": "gpt-5.4", "latency_ms": 4148, "timestamp": "2026-05-23T11:24:40.749304+00:00", "error": null}, "retrieved_context": [{"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 2. Acceptable Primary Identity Documents\n\nA primary identity document must be government-issued, photographic, and contain the holder's full legal name and date of birth. The following are accepted:\n\n| Document Type | Jurisdiction | Conditions |\n|---------------|-------------|------------|\n| Passport | Any | Valid, **or** expired within the last **6 months** |\n| National Identity Card | EU/EEA countries and UK | Must be valid at the time of submission |\n| Biometric Residence Permit | UK | Must be valid at the time of submission |\n| Driving Licence (photocard) | UK, EU/EEA, US, Canada, Australia | Must be valid; paper licences not accepted |\n\n**A document issued more than 10 years ago is not acceptable** as a primary identity document, even if it technically remains valid (this most commonly affects older passports with 10-year validity that are still in date but were issued more than 10 years ago).\n\nOnly one primary identity document is required. If the document is damaged, partially illegible, or of poor quality, the client must be asked to resubmit with a clearer copy or an alternative document.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Acceptable Proof of Address Documents"}, {"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 3. Acceptable Proof of Address Documents\n\nA proof of address document must show the client's full name and current residential address. It must have been **issued within the last 3 months** at the time of submission.\n\nThe following are accepted:\n\n- Bank or building society statement (original or electronic, from a regulated institution)\n- Utility bill (gas, electricity, water, broadband — mobile phone bills are not accepted)\n- Council tax bill (current year)\n- HMRC correspondence (e.g., tax return notice, NI correspondence)\n- Mortgage statement (from a regulated lender)\n- Tenancy agreement (signed and dated, covering the current period)\n\nA **mobile phone bill is not an acceptable proof of address** under any circumstances.\n\nThe proof of address must match the residential address declared by the client. Where there is a discrepancy, the reviewing officer must request an explanation and document it on the file.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Document Age and Validity Rules\n\nThe following rules apply regardless of document type:"}, {"document": "04_document_verification_standards.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 4. Document Age and Validity Rules\n\nThe following rules apply regardless of document type:\n\n| Rule | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| Primary ID validity | Passport: valid or expired within 6 months. National ID / BRP / Driving Licence: must be valid at submission |\n| Primary ID issuance age | Must have been issued within the last 10 years |\n| Proof of address age | Must have been issued within the last 3 months |\n| Supporting SoW documents | Age requirements vary by document type — see Source of Wealth Verification Guidelines (Policy 02) |\n\nWhere a client is unable to provide documents meeting these requirements, the reviewing officer must escalate to the MLRO before proceeding.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Remote Onboarding and Biometric Verification\n\nAll clients onboarding through the platform's remote digital channel are subject to biometric identity verification. This requirement cannot be waived for remote clients.\n\nBiometric verification must:"}]}
{"question": "What documents are required to verify Source of Wealth where a client's wealth comes from employment income?", "response": {"question": "What documents are required to verify Source of Wealth where a client's wealth comes from employment income?", "answer": "- Payslips for the most recent three consecutive months, issued by the employer and showing gross and net pay\n- A letter from the employer confirming the client’s role, salary, and length of employment, on headed paper and signed by HR or a director\n- Bank statements for the most recent three months showing salary credits matching the payslips", "citations": [{"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "If a client's wealth does not fit any of these categories, the case must be escalated to the MLRO before the file progresses. The MLRO will determine whether the wealth source is sufficiently explained and documented and will record the decision.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Documentation Requirements by Category\n\n### 3.1 Employment / Salary\n\nRequired documents:\n\n- Payslips for the most recent **three consecutive months**, issued by the employer and showing gross and net pay\n- A letter from the employer confirming the client's role, salary, and length of employment (on headed paper, signed by HR or a director)\n- Bank statements for the most recent **three months**, showing salary credits matching the payslips\n\nWhere a client is self-employed, the following replace the employer letter and payslips:"}, {"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "# Source of Wealth Verification Guidelines\n**Version:** 1.3  \n**Effective Date:** 1 March 2025  \n**Owner:** MLRO  \n**Review Cycle:** Annual\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nSource of Wealth (SoW) verification establishes how a client accumulated the assets they are bringing to or through the regulated firm. It is distinct from Source of Funds (SoF), which covers where the specific funds in a transaction originate. Both may be required; this document governs SoW.\n\nUnverified or inadequately explained wealth is one of the most common findings in AML regulatory reviews. This policy sets minimum documentation standards for each recognised wealth category and defines when additional corroboration is required.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Recognised Wealth Categories"}, {"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 2. Recognised Wealth Categories\n\nThe platform recognises five primary Source of Wealth categories. Where a client's wealth derives from more than one category, each must be documented and verified independently.\n\n| # | Category | Description |\n|---|----------|-------------|\n| 1 | Employment / Salary | Wealth accumulated through employment income over time |\n| 2 | Business Ownership or Sale | Wealth from ownership of, or proceeds from the sale of, a business |\n| 3 | Inheritance | Wealth received through a bequest, estate distribution or gift |\n| 4 | Investment Portfolio | Wealth accumulated through returns on financial investments |\n| 5 | Property / Real Estate | Wealth accumulated through property ownership, rental income or sale proceeds |"}], "model": "gpt-5.4", "latency_ms": 4208, "timestamp": "2026-05-23T11:29:48.771821+00:00", "error": null}, "retrieved_context": [{"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "If a client's wealth does not fit any of these categories, the case must be escalated to the MLRO before the file progresses. The MLRO will determine whether the wealth source is sufficiently explained and documented and will record the decision.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Documentation Requirements by Category\n\n### 3.1 Employment / Salary\n\nRequired documents:\n\n- Payslips for the most recent **three consecutive months**, issued by the employer and showing gross and net pay\n- A letter from the employer confirming the client's role, salary, and length of employment (on headed paper, signed by HR or a director)\n- Bank statements for the most recent **three months**, showing salary credits matching the payslips\n\nWhere a client is self-employed, the following replace the employer letter and payslips:"}, {"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "# Source of Wealth Verification Guidelines\n**Version:** 1.3  \n**Effective Date:** 1 March 2025  \n**Owner:** MLRO  \n**Review Cycle:** Annual\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nSource of Wealth (SoW) verification establishes how a client accumulated the assets they are bringing to or through the regulated firm. It is distinct from Source of Funds (SoF), which covers where the specific funds in a transaction originate. Both may be required; this document governs SoW.\n\nUnverified or inadequately explained wealth is one of the most common findings in AML regulatory reviews. This policy sets minimum documentation standards for each recognised wealth category and defines when additional corroboration is required.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Recognised Wealth Categories"}, {"document": "02_source_of_wealth_verification_guidelines.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 2. Recognised Wealth Categories\n\nThe platform recognises five primary Source of Wealth categories. Where a client's wealth derives from more than one category, each must be documented and verified independently.\n\n| # | Category | Description |\n|---|----------|-------------|\n| 1 | Employment / Salary | Wealth accumulated through employment income over time |\n| 2 | Business Ownership or Sale | Wealth from ownership of, or proceeds from the sale of, a business |\n| 3 | Inheritance | Wealth received through a bequest, estate distribution or gift |\n| 4 | Investment Portfolio | Wealth accumulated through returns on financial investments |\n| 5 | Property / Real Estate | Wealth accumulated through property ownership, rental income or sale proceeds |"}]}
{"question": "How frequently must sanctions screening be repeated for existing medium-risk clients?", "response": {"question": "How frequently must sanctions screening be repeated for existing medium-risk clients?", "answer": "Quarterly. In addition, sanctions list updates trigger an immediate re-screen of all active clients within 24 hours of the update being published.", "citations": [{"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "1. All sanctions lists defined in Section 2.1\n2. The global PEP database\n3. Adverse media database (minimum 12-month lookback)\n\nScreening must be documented with:\n\n- The date and time of screening\n- The screening provider and list versions used\n- The result (No Match / Potential Match / Confirmed Match)\n- Where a potential match is identified: the match details and the reviewing officer's disposition\n\nNo client file may proceed to the CDD completion stage until screening is documented as either No Match or a confirmed Potential Match that has been reviewed and dispositioned.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Ongoing Periodic Screening\n\nScreening is not a one-time event. All active client relationships are subject to ongoing screening at intervals determined by the client's risk band:"}, {"document": "05_risk_assessment_framework.md", "chunk": "If EDD cannot be completed to the required standard, the relationship must not be established. If it arises mid-relationship, the relationship must be suspended and the MLRO notified.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Risk Score Reassessment\n\nThe risk score is reassessed at each scheduled periodic review and must also be reassessed immediately when:\n\n- The client reports a change of address, nationality, or beneficial ownership\n- A new sanctions, PEP, or adverse media alert is generated\n- The client requests a new or materially different service\n- A transaction pattern inconsistent with the client's profile is identified"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 4. Ongoing Periodic Screening\n\nScreening is not a one-time event. All active client relationships are subject to ongoing screening at intervals determined by the client's risk band:\n\n| Risk Band | Screening Frequency |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| Low (0–30) | Annually |\n| Medium (31–60) | Quarterly |\n| High (61–100) | Monthly |\n\nIn addition to scheduled periodic screening, the platform operates **continuous monitoring** for sanctions list updates. Any addition or amendment to a sanctions list triggers an immediate re-screen of all active clients within **24 hours** of the list update being published.\n\nPEP database updates trigger an immediate re-screen of all active clients within **48 hours**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Alert Management\n\n### 5.1 False Positives"}], "model": "gpt-5.4", "latency_ms": 3902, "timestamp": "2026-05-23T11:30:34.557630+00:00", "error": null}, "retrieved_context": [{"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "1. All sanctions lists defined in Section 2.1\n2. The global PEP database\n3. Adverse media database (minimum 12-month lookback)\n\nScreening must be documented with:\n\n- The date and time of screening\n- The screening provider and list versions used\n- The result (No Match / Potential Match / Confirmed Match)\n- Where a potential match is identified: the match details and the reviewing officer's disposition\n\nNo client file may proceed to the CDD completion stage until screening is documented as either No Match or a confirmed Potential Match that has been reviewed and dispositioned.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Ongoing Periodic Screening\n\nScreening is not a one-time event. All active client relationships are subject to ongoing screening at intervals determined by the client's risk band:"}, {"document": "05_risk_assessment_framework.md", "chunk": "If EDD cannot be completed to the required standard, the relationship must not be established. If it arises mid-relationship, the relationship must be suspended and the MLRO notified.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Risk Score Reassessment\n\nThe risk score is reassessed at each scheduled periodic review and must also be reassessed immediately when:\n\n- The client reports a change of address, nationality, or beneficial ownership\n- A new sanctions, PEP, or adverse media alert is generated\n- The client requests a new or materially different service\n- A transaction pattern inconsistent with the client's profile is identified"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "---\n\n## 4. Ongoing Periodic Screening\n\nScreening is not a one-time event. All active client relationships are subject to ongoing screening at intervals determined by the client's risk band:\n\n| Risk Band | Screening Frequency |\n|-----------|-------------------|\n| Low (0–30) | Annually |\n| Medium (31–60) | Quarterly |\n| High (61–100) | Monthly |\n\nIn addition to scheduled periodic screening, the platform operates **continuous monitoring** for sanctions list updates. Any addition or amendment to a sanctions list triggers an immediate re-screen of all active clients within **24 hours** of the list update being published.\n\nPEP database updates trigger an immediate re-screen of all active clients within **48 hours**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Alert Management\n\n### 5.1 False Positives"}]}
{"question": "A client has been identified as a foreign PEP and also scores 72 on the risk assessment. What requirements apply?", "response": {"question": "A client has been identified as a foreign PEP and also scores 72 on the risk assessment. What requirements apply?", "answer": "The client is subject to PEP requirements and high-risk treatment based on the score of 72.\n\nApplicable requirements from the provided documents:\n- Enhanced Due Diligence is automatically triggered because the client is a PEP.\n- Senior management approval is required before the relationship is established or continued.\n- The basis for proceeding must be documented and signed off by a senior manager.\n- The relationship must be reviewed at least every six months.\n- Source of Wealth verification must meet the two-source corroboration standard applicable to EDD clients.\n\nThe provided documents do not define any additional procedure or threshold-based requirements specifically for a risk assessment score of 72.", "citations": [{"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "**Close associates and family members of PEPs** are subject to the same screening and enhanced diligence requirements as the PEP themselves. Close associates include: spouse or civil partner, children and their spouses, parents, and known business partners.\n\nA PEP who left public office more than **12 months ago** transitions to standard screening unless the MLRO determines residual risk warrants continued enhanced treatment.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Initial Screening at Onboarding\n\nEvery client, beneficial owner, director, and authorised signatory must be screened before the business relationship is established. Screening must be completed against:\n\n1. All sanctions lists defined in Section 2.1\n2. The global PEP database\n3. Adverse media database (minimum 12-month lookback)"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "Documentation of all steps taken must be created contemporaneously and stored on the client file. Records must not be altered after the fact.\n\n---\n\n## 6. PEP-Specific Requirements\n\nWhere a client is identified as a PEP (at any tier), regardless of the outcome of sanctions screening:\n\n- Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) is automatically triggered (see CDD Policy, Section 5)\n- Senior management approval is required before the relationship is established or continued\n- The basis for proceeding must be documented and signed off by a senior manager\n- The relationship is subject to six-monthly review as a minimum\n- Source of Wealth verification must meet the two-source corroboration standard applicable to EDD clients"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "Screening must be conducted against all active lists simultaneously. A client is considered sanctioned if they appear on any list, regardless of which jurisdiction imposed the measure.\n\n### 2.2 Politically Exposed Person (PEP)\n\nA Politically Exposed Person is an individual who holds, or has held within the last **12 months**, a prominent public function. The platform recognises three PEP tiers:"}], "model": "gpt-5.4", "latency_ms": 5003, "timestamp": "2026-05-23T11:31:33.713716+00:00", "error": null}, "retrieved_context": [{"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "**Close associates and family members of PEPs** are subject to the same screening and enhanced diligence requirements as the PEP themselves. Close associates include: spouse or civil partner, children and their spouses, parents, and known business partners.\n\nA PEP who left public office more than **12 months ago** transitions to standard screening unless the MLRO determines residual risk warrants continued enhanced treatment.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Initial Screening at Onboarding\n\nEvery client, beneficial owner, director, and authorised signatory must be screened before the business relationship is established. Screening must be completed against:\n\n1. All sanctions lists defined in Section 2.1\n2. The global PEP database\n3. Adverse media database (minimum 12-month lookback)"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "Documentation of all steps taken must be created contemporaneously and stored on the client file. Records must not be altered after the fact.\n\n---\n\n## 6. PEP-Specific Requirements\n\nWhere a client is identified as a PEP (at any tier), regardless of the outcome of sanctions screening:\n\n- Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) is automatically triggered (see CDD Policy, Section 5)\n- Senior management approval is required before the relationship is established or continued\n- The basis for proceeding must be documented and signed off by a senior manager\n- The relationship is subject to six-monthly review as a minimum\n- Source of Wealth verification must meet the two-source corroboration standard applicable to EDD clients"}, {"document": "03_sanctions_and_pep_screening_procedure.md", "chunk": "Screening must be conducted against all active lists simultaneously. A client is considered sanctioned if they appear on any list, regardless of which jurisdiction imposed the measure.\n\n### 2.2 Politically Exposed Person (PEP)\n\nA Politically Exposed Person is an individual who holds, or has held within the last **12 months**, a prominent public function. The platform recognises three PEP tiers:"}]}
